Intent has a half-life. Our call strategy gave a new lead its final attempt on day 14, and by then most had moved on. Moving the last call to day 7 nearly doubled the share of leads reached in their first week, from **23.2% to 45.0%**.

I compared strict 14-day cohorts either side of the change at one university partner, with tiers held constant. Overall contact rate rose from **45.5% to 55.3%**. Of the leads we contacted, **12.0% applied within 21 days**, up from 8.7%, a **38% relative lift**. Average attempts per contacted lead rose from 2.09 to 3.36. Same effort, spent while the lead still cared. Operationally the change held too: late tasks dropped and the average call cycle shortened from 10.5 days to 3.2.

One honest caveat made the result credible rather than convenient. The before period carried a dialler backlog, so part of the improvement was operational recovery, not strategy. That went in the body of the write-up, not a footnote. The recommendation survived the caveat, and compressing the window became the playbook offered to other partners: spend attempts early, measure applications rather than activity.
